@page "/mechanism/3-in-process"

<PageTitle>3. IJSInProcessObjectReference — JS interop demos</PageTitle>

<div class="mechanism-page">
    <MechanismNav />

    <header class="mech-head">
        <p class="mech-kicker">
            1.3 · Synchronous calls — you are reading this in
            <span class="host-chip">@RuntimeMode.Name</span> — the same page in
            <CounterpartLink />
        </p>
        <h1>IJSInProcessObjectReference: synchronous interop</h1>
        <p class="mech-lede">
            While .NET runs in the browser it can call JavaScript without awaiting anything: no task,
            no round trip. Getting the reference is still awaited, because import is a browser
            operation. Every call after that one is not.
        </p>
        <p class="demo-support">
            Available in: <strong>@Mechanisms.Availability("3-in-process")</strong>
        </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>What "in process" is worth, and what it costs</h2>
        <p>
            The page has one thread. In WebAssembly, .NET and JavaScript share it, so a synchronous
            call is a direct hand-off — no JSON protocol, no scheduler hop. It is also a monopoly:
            while the call runs, nothing else in the tab can run, including the code that paints the
            screen.
        </p>
        <p>
            The ✗ panel below measures that instead of claiming it. A <code>requestAnimationFrame</code>
            loop records the longest gap between two painted frames while a synchronous
            <code>blockMainThread(1200)</code> busy-waits.
        </p>
        @if (RuntimeMode.IsWebAssembly)
        {
            <p class="mech-note">
                The measurement needs a tab that is actually painting. Run it in a hidden or
                minimised tab and <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> never fires, so the panel reports
                a gap of <strong>0 ms</strong> next to a call that really did hold the thread for
                1200 ms. Focus the window before you read the number.
            </p>
        }
    </section>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>What the panels print in @RuntimeMode.Name</h2>
        @if (RuntimeMode.IsWebAssembly)
        {
            <p class="mech-out-label">✓ Synchronous Invoke, no await</p>
            <pre class="mech-out">Hello from the ES module — call #1. The counter survived the previous call. — returned by Invoke&lt;string&gt;(), with no await on this call.</pre>

            <p class="mech-out-label">✗ Synchronous call into slow JavaScript</p>
            <pre class="mech-out mech-out-fail">the synchronous call held the thread for 1200 ms; the longest gap between
two painted frames was 1298 ms. A page that is not blocked paints every ~16 ms.</pre>

            <p class="mech-note">
                On this host the ✓ panel works, so the interesting one is the ✗ panel: it is not a
                mistake in the API, it is the API used against JavaScript that is too slow to be
                called synchronously. Run the same page in Blazor Server and the second panel is
                replaced by the failure described below.
            </p>
        }
        else
        {
            <p class="mech-out-label">✓ Synchronous Invoke, no await — the panel has no button here</p>
            <pre class="mech-out mech-out-flat">Cannot run here: this API does not exist in Blazor Server.</pre>

            <p class="mech-out-label">✗ Ask the Server runtime for an in-process reference</p>
            <pre class="mech-out mech-out-fail">JSException: An exception occurred executing JS interop: Deserialization of
interface or abstract types is not supported. Type
'Microsoft.JSInterop.IJSInProcessObjectReference'. Path: $ | LineNumber: 0 |
BytePositionInLine: 1.. See InnerException for more details.
  caused by NotSupportedException: Deserialization of interface or abstract
types is not supported. Type 'Microsoft.JSInterop.IJSInProcessObjectReference'.</pre>

            <p class="mech-note">
                Read what is <strong>not</strong> in that message: there is no "this platform does not
                support that" anywhere in it. The runtime never reaches a capability check — it asks
                the JavaScript side for a value, cannot deserialise the type you declared, and gives
                you a JSON-shaped complaint instead. The C# is legal and compiles clean on any target,
                which is exactly why you build and run the Server host rather than trusting the WASM
                build.
            </p>
        }
    </section>

    <section class="mech-split">
        <div class="mech-use">
            <h2>Use it when</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>You are in WebAssembly and a tight client-side loop is dominated by async
                    overhead — measured, not assumed.</li>
                <li>The JavaScript returns in well under a millisecond and you call it thousands of
                    times per frame.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="mech-never">
            <h2>Never when</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>You run on Blazor Server — synchronous calls are WASM only.</li>
                <li>The JavaScript function is slow — you will freeze the UI thread.</li>
                <li>You are not in a measured performance-critical loop — async is safer and works
                    everywhere.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <h2 class="mech-live-head">The live panels</h2>
    <CascadingValue Name="MechanismPageOwnsHeading" Value="true">
        <Demo03InProcess />
    </CascadingValue>

    <MechanismPager Slug="3-in-process" />
</div>
